.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt NVME 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvme
.Nd NVM Express disk driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/diskN
.Pp
.Pa /dev/diskNpM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver drives each NVM Express controller on the PCI bus, up to four,
through an admin queue and one I/O queue pair.
Each active namespace with 512-byte blocks and no metadata becomes a disk,
up to four a controller, with the nodes
.Xr disk 4
describes.
The kernel probes AHCI, then NVMe, then ATA, and the first disk found is
disk 0.
Partitions, mounts, a root selected by
.Ql rach2.root=
and a boot partition selected by
.Ql rach2.boot=
take a namespace as they take a SATA disk.
.Pp
.Pa /proc/disk
lists a namespace with transport
.Ql nvme
and a location of the controller's number times four plus the namespace's
place among those the controller published.
Its model, serial number and firmware revision are the controller's.
.Pp
The boot reads and writes by polling the completion queue.
Once the root is mounted, each controller is moved to its MSI-X message,
or to MSI when it has no MSI-X table, on a vector of its own; a transfer
then sleeps its caller until the message, and a page fault with
interrupts off still polls.
.Pa /proc/interrupts
lists the vector as
.Ql nvme .
The controller keeps polling when the kernel command line has
.Ql rach2.nvmepoll ,
when the local APIC is not in use, when no vector is free, and when the
controller has neither MSI-X nor MSI.
.Pp
A write is sent with force unit access, so it is on the medium when it
returns.
A file system that writes several blocks and flushes once after them
sends them without it, and its flush is the controller's Flush command.
.Pp
The metal kernel's read-only policy, which
.Xr disk 7
describes, judges an NVMe command by the ATA command it corresponds to: a
read is allowed, and a write and a flush are refused.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The driver writes these lines to the kernel log, with
.Ar N
the controller's number and
.Ar M
the namespace identifier.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql nvme: N: unusable: reason
The controller does not become ready, reports a fatal status, lacks the
NVM command set, has no page size of 4 KiB, does not answer or refuses an
admin command, or has registers the kernel cannot map.
It is left disabled and the boot goes on.
.It Ql nvme: N: namespace M: refused: reason
The namespace has blocks of another size than 512 bytes, 4096 among
them, or metadata, or did not answer Identify; it is not published.
.It Ql nvme: N: namespace M: command 0xC failed: status 0xS
The controller failed the command, and the transfer fails with
.Er EIO .
.It Ql nvme: N: namespace M: command 0xC failed: no answer in thirty seconds
The controller did not answer within the wait, five seconds with
interrupts off.
The transfer fails with
.Er EIO ,
and the controller is reset and its queues created again; every request
it held fails with
.Er EIO .
A controller that does not stop for the reset stops the kernel with a
panic, since it may still write into memory given back to its owner.
.It Ql nvme: N: recovery: failed: reason; the controller is out of use
The controller did not come back from the reset, and every later transfer
to its namespaces fails with
.Er EIO .
.It Ql nvme: interrupts: messages on vector V, msi-x, K disks queued
The controller was moved to its messages, MSI-X or MSI.
.It Ql nvme: polled: reason
The controller keeps polling, for the reason given.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 4 ,
.Xr proc 5 ,
.Xr disk 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The commands and registers are those of the NVM Express Base
Specification, revision 1.4.
.Sh CAVEATS
Linux names a namespace
.Pa /dev/nvmeCnN
and its partitions
.Pa /dev/nvmeCnNpP ,
gives each controller a character device for passing commands through, and
takes namespaces of 4096-byte blocks.
Rach2 names a namespace as a disk, has no pass-through, and refuses
4096-byte blocks: the partition table on such a disk, and the firmware that
reads it, count in its blocks, and Rach2's block layer counts in 512-byte
sectors.
Linux makes an I/O queue pair for each processor and tells the controller
of a power-off; Rach2 makes one pair and sends no shutdown notification,
so the controller counts each power-off as an unsafe shutdown, though
every write it answered with force unit access or flushed is on the
medium.
